.TH CJSON_GET 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cjson_get \- navigate to a DOM node with a dot-separated path
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cjson.h>

cjson cjson_get(cjson root, const char *path);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Navigates from root through the components of path.  It returns the
node that the last component addresses.

path is a dot-separated sequence of component strings.  A component
can start with '#' and then hold one or more decimal digits.  Such a
component addresses an array element by a zero-based index when the
current node is CJSON_LIST.  In every other case the macro uses the
whole component as a literal object key, and the '#' is part of that
key.  A bare '#' with no digits after it on a CJSON_LIST node is an
error, and the macro returns NULL.

A NULL root gives NULL.  A NULL path or an empty path gives root
unchanged.  An empty component in a path addresses nothing, and the
macro then gives NULL.  A leading dot, a trailing dot and two dots
together (as in "a..b") make an empty component.

Escape sequences in path components:
  \e.   -> a literal '.' in the key (not treated as a path separator)
  \e\e   -> a literal '\e' in the key
The macro leaves a '\e' before any other character unchanged.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
root   Root cjson node (typically CJSON_DICTIONARY or CJSON_LIST).
path   Dot-separated path string, for example
       "users.#0.address.city".
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A cjson handle to the target node.  The caller does not own it.
The macro returns NULL when a component along the path is missing.
It also returns NULL when the type of the current node does not
support the asked-for step.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The handle that the macro returns becomes invalid when the caller
changes the tree or destroys it.  Do not free that handle.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cjson city = cjson_get(doc, "users.#0.address.city");
if (cjson_type(city) == CJSON_STRING)
    printf("%s\en", cjson_str_val(city));

/* '#0' in an object context is a literal key, not an index */
cjson v = cjson_get(doc, "#0");
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_set (3),
.BR cjson_type (3)
